# Migrating `TimedExecutor` to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET 5+
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` is obsolete** and non-functional
3. **`ThreadAbortException` is never thrown** by the runtime

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            if (task.Wait(timeout))
                return task.Result; // unwraps AggregateException if faulted

            cts.Cancel();
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            // Preserve original exception behavior
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

## If the work supports cooperative cancellation (preferred)

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var token = cts.Token;

        try
        {
            return Task.Run(() => work(token), token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

## Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **Fire-and-forget threads** | Unlike `Thread.Abort()`, cancellation is *cooperative*. If `work` ignores the token, it keeps running after timeout. |
| **`Task.Result` vs `.GetAwaiter().GetResult()`** | `Result` wraps in `AggregateException`; `GetAwaiter().GetResult()` throws the inner exception directly. |
| **Resource leaks** | The old code killed the thread; the new code cannot force-stop uncooperative work. Wrap long-running I/O with cancellation-aware APIs. |
| **Deadlocks** | Calling `.Result`/`.Wait()` on a UI or ASP.NET sync context can deadlock. Use `async`/`await` when possible. |

## For truly uncooperative code (last resort)

If you absolutely must kill runaway work (e.g., calling third-party code that doesn't accept a `CancellationToken`), isolate it in a **separate process** and kill the process on timeout. In-process forced termination is no longer supported.
